# Draft OpenDir release

When the user says **draft a release** (or similar), **you** draft the release. Execute the full workflow end-to-end and create the draft on GitHub with **`gh`**. Do not respond with instructions for the user to run `gh`, `git push`, or the GitHub UI unless you have exhausted every available tool and retry and still cannot reach GitHub.

**Never** treat “draft the release” as a documentation or handoff task. The expected outcome is a draft release existing on GitHub.

## Version bump

Read `opendir-extension/package.json`, then bump using the patch rule in `opendir-extension/scripts/version.mjs`:

- Increment **only** the patch segment by 1: `0.1.8` → `0.1.9` → `0.1.10`
- Run `version:bump` **once per release commit** — never twice in the same commit or push

Run:

```bash
cd opendir-extension
npm run version:bump
```

`package.json` is the canonical version. `bump-version.mjs` updates it and syncs `manifest.json` so Chrome and the GitHub tag (`v0.0.2`) always match. Every `npm run build` re-syncs manifest from package.json and writes that version into `dist/manifest.json`.

Update `CHANGELOG.md` with the new version section before committing.

## Build and package

```bash
cd opendir-extension
npm test
npm run build
node scripts/package-release.mjs
```

This produces `release/OpenDir-<version>.zip`.

## Commit, push, draft with gh (required)

1. Commit and push the working branch (`git push -u origin <branch>`, retry with backoff on network errors).
2. Delete any obsolete draft for the same or superseded tag if needed (`gh release delete … --yes`).
3. Create the draft release:

```bash
gh release create v<version> \
  --draft \
  --title "OpenDir <version>" \
  --target <branch-name> \
  --notes-file opendir-extension/RELEASE.md \
  release/OpenDir-<version>.zip
```

Use `--draft` unless the user explicitly asks to publish.

## If GitHub access fails

Retry push and `gh` (including re-auth paths available in the environment). Do **not** default to telling the user to do your job. Only after repeated failures, report what blocked you and continue attempting resolution in follow-up turns — still without substituting a “run this locally” checklist for completing the task.
